:: Day2_Body_Inventory [warrior_queen_arc day2]

/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
/*  THE BLADE REFORGED — Day 2: The Body Inventory            */
/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* ── SCENE 1: BEFORE DAWN — MAERA ── */

I am already awake when the darkness begin to thin. I have not slept — not truly. The night was a restless drift between half-dreams and the strange, constant awareness of my own body beneath the sheets. Every time I shifted, the nightgown reminded me. The brush of linen against tender chest. The press of thighs that no longer part the way they used to. The ache in my hips, deep and slow, like the bones themselves are whispering to each other in a language I do not speak.

I rise and cross to the wardrobe in the grey pre-light. My hands are reaching for the newer tunic — the dark one, the forgiving one — when a knock come at my door. Lighter than Aldric's. Quicker. Three taps and then a fourth, impatient.

<<speech "maera">>If you're awake, I'm coming in. If you're not, I'm still coming in.<</speech>>

The door open before I can answer. Maera slip through the gap like a cat through a fence — all quick movements and sharp eyes, her dark curls still wild from her own bed, a bundle of fabric tucked under one arm.

She stop when she see me.

I am standing in the nightgown, one hand on the wardrobe door, bare-legged, my hair falling past my jaw in tangles. The morning cold has done its work — the thin linen cling to every contour, and the small swellings at my chest are... visible. Unmistakably, embarrassingly visible, the nipples peaked against the fabric like two accusations I cannot deny.

Maera's eyes travel down. Then up. Her mouth open. Close. Open again.

<<speech "maera">>Well. Good morning to you too.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Maera—<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>No, no, don't cover yourself, you look like a startled deer and it's too early for that. I brought you something.<</speech>>

She toss the bundle onto my bed and unfold it with brisk, practical hands. Inside: a length of soft, tightly woven cloth, about the width of my forearm, with ties at each end.

<<speech "mc">>What is that?<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>A breast band. Before you say a word — Veletia didn't send me. I came on my own. I watched you at dinner last night, the way you kept crossing your arms, pulling your tunic, hunching your shoulders like you were trying to fold yourself in half. You looked miserable. So I went to the linen stores and made this. It's what the Shield Maidens wear under training gear. Flat, snug, keep everything... still.<</speech>>

She hold it up and look at me with that expression she wear when she has decided something and the world can either agree or get out of her way.

<<speech "maera">>Arms up.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>I can dress myself, Maera.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>You can barely reach the top shelf anymore with those shrinking shoulders. Arms up.<</speech>>

I raise my arms.

She step close and begin to wrap the band around my chest, over the nightgown at first, testing the fit. Her hands are quick and impersonal, the efficiency of someone who has laced corsets and bound wounds. But when her palm press flat against the side of my chest to smooth the fabric, she pause. Just for a heartbeat. Her fingers resting against the curve that has no business being there — the soft, warm, undeniable swell of new breast tissue beneath thin linen.

<<speech "maera">>They've grown since last week.<</speech>>

She say it quietly. Not mocking. Not fascinated. Just... noting. The way one might note that the season has turned.

<<speech "mc">>I know.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>Does it hurt?<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Not pain. More like... pressure. Tenderness. Like a bruise that never quite form, just sit there humming.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>Sounds like what my sister said when hers came in. She was thirteen. Used to sleep on her stomach and wake up wanting to scream.<</speech>>

I stare at her. The comparison land somewhere in my chest that has nothing to do with the physical changes.

<<speech "mc">>I am not your thirteen-year-old sister, Maera.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>No. You're a twenty-year-old prince who is growing tits and has sword training in half an hour. Which is considerably worse. Now take the nightgown off so I can bind you properly.<</speech>>

I hesitate. She has seen me shirtless a hundred times — we grew up together, bathing in the same river, running half-clothed through summer gardens. But that was before. Before the alchemy carved new topography into my chest. Before looking down at myself became an act requiring courage.

<<speech "maera">>Caelan. It's me.<</speech>>

Her voice is soft now. The sharpness gone. Just Maera, who once punched a nobleman's son for calling me soft, who has slept outside my door during fevers, who know me better than anyone in this castle except perhaps my father.

I pull the nightgown over my head.

The cold air hit my bare skin and everything tighten — the new flesh drawing up, the nipples hardening instantly, gooseflesh racing across the soft curve of what is growing there. I stand with my arms at my sides, my jaw set, my eyes fixed on the far wall, and I let her see.

Maera look. She is not cruel about it and she is not gentle about it. She simply look, the way a soldier assess a field — noting terrain, measuring distances, planning a path forward.

<<speech "maera">>The left one's slightly bigger than the right. That's normal, by the way. No woman is perfectly matched. My right is bigger than my left and it drive me mad when I lace a bodice.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Maera.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>I'm being helpful. Hold still.<</speech>>

She wrap the breast band directly against my skin. The fabric is firm but not painful — compressing the new tissue flat against my chest, holding it still, preventing the bounce and sway that had tormented me yesterday. She tie the ends at my back with a neat knot.

<<speech "maera">>Breathe.<</speech>>

I breathe. The band is tight but bearable. When I move — twist my torso, raise my arms, bend forward — the chest stay still. No jostling. No pulling. No traitorous outline against fabric.

<<speech "maera">>Better?<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>...Yes. Much better. Thank you.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>Don't thank me. Just don't get killed in training. I'm not making a funeral dress for someone with your measurements — I wouldn't know where to start.<</speech>>

She grin — that sharp, crooked grin that has been the fixed point of my life since we were children — and duck out the door before I can respond.

I finish dressing in silence. The tunic fall properly now, the band hiding what need to be hidden, the fabric draping over a shape that is... manageable. Not invisible. But manageable.

I lace my boots. Tie my hair back — it is long enough now to gather at the nape of my neck, though half of it escape immediately. I look at the mirror.

The person looking back could be a young man. Could be a young woman. Could be something the world has not yet found a name for. The breast band flatten the chest but the waist still narrow, the hips still flare, the jaw still soften. The eyes are wider than they were a year ago. The lips fuller. The skin smoother.

I turn away and head for the Iron Garden.


/* ── SCENE 2: THE IRON GARDEN — ALDRIC'S INSPECTION ── */

Aldric is where he was yesterday. Centre of the yard. Arms behind his back. As though he has not moved in the intervening hours, as though he has been standing here in the dark like a gargoyle waiting for dawn to give him purpose.

But he is not alone.

To his left, a wooden table has been set up. On it: a length of measuring cord, a pot of charcoal, a roll of parchment, and what appear to be several leather straps of varying lengths. The sight of it make something cold settle in my stomach.

<<speech "aldric">>You're on time. That is an improvement. Small, but measurable. Speaking of which—<</speech>>

He gesture toward the table.

<<speech "aldric">>Today we do not train. Today we take inventory.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Inventory of what?<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Of you. Of every change. Every shift, every gain, every loss. I cannot build a fighting style for a body I do not understand. So today I learn your body the way I learn any weapon — by measuring it, testing it, finding where it is strong and where it will break.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Aldric, I am not comfortable with—<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Comfort is not a word that exist in this yard. You want comfort, go to Veletia. She'll wrap you in silk and tell you how lovely you look. I will tell you the truth, and the truth require numbers.<</speech>>

He pick up the measuring cord.

<<speech "aldric">>Stand in the centre. Feet together. Arms out to the sides.<</speech>>

I walk to the centre of the yard and assume the position. The morning sun is just cresting the eastern wall, casting long shadows across the packed earth. I can feel the breast band tight against my chest. I can feel the breeches pressing against my hips. I can feel every inch of this body that is becoming a territory I do not fully control.

Aldric approach with the cord and begin to measure.

He start with my wingspan — fingertip to fingertip, arms extended. He stretch the cord, mark it with charcoal on the parchment.

<<speech "aldric">>Down three inches from six months ago. Your arms are shorter relative to your height. Or rather, your proportions are shifting — torso lengthening slightly, arms drawing in. You will have less reach with a longsword. That matter.<</speech>>

He measure my shoulders.

<<speech "aldric">>Narrower by a full hand's width since the start of the potions. The clavicle is restructuring. I can see it — the angle has changed. Your old breastplate would sit on you like a bucket on a fencepost now.<</speech>>

My waist.

He wrap the cord around my midsection and I feel it tighten against the new inward curve. His hands are professional, detached, but when the cord settle into the narrowest point — lower than a man's waist, higher than the hips, in that specifically feminine indentation that has been deepening week by week — he pause for a moment, reading the measurement twice.

<<speech "aldric">>Four inches off the waist since we last fitted your armor. Four inches. Do you understand what that mean in combat terms? Your center mass has compressed. Your core is smaller. That is both a weakness and a strength, but only if we know how to use it.<</speech>>

Then the hips.

Aldric wrap the cord around the widest point of my pelvis. The cord settle against the breeches and I feel it press into the new breadth — the bone that has spread, the flesh that has padded, the whole architecture of my lower body that is rebuilding itself into something designed for purposes far removed from war.

<<speech "aldric">>Three inches on the hips since last measurement. Your waist-to-hip ratio is approaching... well.<</speech>>

He consult the parchment.

<<speech "aldric">>You now have a waist-to-hip ratio comparable to most women in the garrison. The Shield Maidens, for reference. Your body is distributing weight to the hips and thighs, which lower your centre of gravity significantly. That is why you stumbled yesterday — you are trying to fight from a centre that no longer exist.<</speech>>

He step back and look at me. The full picture.

<<speech "aldric">>You've lost reach. You've lost shoulder power. You've lost upper body mass. In exchange, you have gained a lower centre of gravity, increased hip flexibility — I can see it in how you stand, your hips rotate more freely now — and I suspect your reflexes have quickened, though we will test that.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>You speak of my body as though it is a ledger. Gains and losses in neat columns.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Because that is exactly what it is. A warrior's body is not a temple, Your Grace. It is a tool. And a tool that has been reshaped must be recalibrated. Sentiment will not save your life. Numbers might.<</speech>>

He is not wrong. I hate that he is not wrong.


/* ── SCENE 3: THE RUNNING TEST ── */

<<speech "aldric">>Now. I need to see you move. Ten laps of the garden perimeter. Full pace. Go.<</speech>>

I begin to run.

The first lap is fine. My legs carry me smoothly enough — the muscles are leaner but not weak, and the longer stride I once had has been replaced by something quicker, choppier, more frequent footfalls. I feel like a different animal. Less horse, more deer. The ground pass beneath me in rapid intervals.

The second lap. My breathing quicken — that shallow, compressed rhythm that I am learning to live with. The breast band hold firm, thank the gods and Maera both, but I can still *feel* the weight on my chest, the slight forward pull with each stride. It is not bouncing, not with the band, but it is *there* — a presence, a mass that shift with my momentum, and my body compensate unconsciously, my spine arching slightly, my shoulders rolling back to counterbalance.

Third lap. My hips. The motion that Aldric spoke of yesterday — the lateral sway, the feminine gait — it amplify when I run. My pelvis rock with each stride, a rolling motion that is more pronounced than any man's run, driven by the wider set of my hip joints and the changed angle of my femurs. I can feel it in my glutes, in muscles I did not know I had, firing in patterns that are new and strange and unsettlingly efficient.

Fourth lap. Heat build in my core. Sweat begin to prick at my skin — and the sweat is different too. Lighter. Less acrid. A change I have noticed in recent weeks but never thought about during exertion. My skin flush pink rather than the ruddy red it used to turn.

Fifth lap. The breast band begin to chafe. The edge of the fabric rub against the side of my chest where new tissue press outward, a friction burn that build from nothing to a persistent, stinging irritation. I grit my teeth and keep running.

Sixth lap. A voice from the garden gate.

<<speech "veletia">>Oh my — is that our prince running laps? How *delightful*! And in those dreadful breeches, no less. We really must discuss proper training attire, Caelan. A lady does not—<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Veletia. Not now.<</speech>>

<<speech "veletia">>I was merely observing! One does not need an invitation to observe.<</speech>>

I round the corner and catch a glimpse of Veletia leaning against the garden archway in a cascade of emerald silk, a cup of morning tea in one hand, watching me with the expression of a woman who has found entertainment and intend to extract every drop of it.

Seventh lap. My legs burn. The muscles in my inner thighs — thicker now, padded with a layer of softness that I am not accustomed to — rub together at the top with each stride. The friction is warm, insistent, maddening. This never happened before. The gap that once existed between my thighs has been filled by the redistribution of fat and the widening of my hips, and now every step is accompanied by this soft, whispering contact of flesh against flesh.

Eighth lap. I pass Veletia again. She sip her tea and call out:

<<speech "veletia">>Your hair is falling out of that tie, darling! And you really must learn to run from the hips — you're fighting your own body!<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>VELETIA.<</speech>>

<<speech "veletia">>Running from the hips is sound advice, Sir Aldric! Ask any woman!<</speech>>

Ninth lap. She is right. Damn her silk-wrapped soul, she is right. I have been fighting my hips — bracing against the sway, stiffening my core, trying to run the way I always have. But my always no longer exist. On this lap, I let go. I let the hips roll. I let the pelvis lead. I stop fighting the rhythm and let it carry me.

The effect is immediate. My stride smooth out. The jarring impact that has been shooting up through my knees ease into something fluid. My speed increase. My breathing settle. It is as though my body has been waiting for me to stop arguing with it.

Tenth lap. I round the final corner at full pace — faster than any of the previous laps — and skid to a stop before Aldric, bending double, hands on my knees, gasping.

He look at the parchment where he has been marking my lap times.

<<speech "aldric">>Your first five laps were slower than your old pace by a significant margin. Your last five were faster. The ninth and tenth were faster than your personal best from before the potions began.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>How is that possible? I've lost muscle. I weigh less. I should be slower.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>You weigh less, which mean less mass to move. Your legs are longer relative to your body now. Your hip flexibility allow a more efficient stride. And on those last two laps, you stopped fighting the gait your body want and let it run its own way. Your body already know how to move. You have simply been too stubborn to listen.<</speech>>

From the archway, Veletia raise her teacup in a mock toast.

<<speech "veletia">>What did I say? The hips, darling. Always the hips.<</speech>>


/* ── SCENE 4: FLEXIBILITY AND REACH ── */

Aldric move to the next test. He plant two wooden posts in the ground, three feet apart, with a rope strung between them at varying heights.

<<speech "aldric">>Duck under. Then step over. Then duck under again. Repeat until I tell you to stop.<</speech>>

The rope is set at chest height first. I duck beneath it — and my body fold differently than it used to. My spine curve with a fluidity that surprise me, the vertebrae articulating in a sequence that feel almost liquid. The narrower shoulders pass beneath the rope with room to spare. Three months ago I would have had to turn sideways.

<<speech "aldric">>Lower.<</speech>>

He drop the rope to waist height. I duck again. My back arch deeper — a deep, sinuous curve that start at my neck and flow through my entire spine. My chest nearly brush the ground but the breast band keep everything contained. My hips rotate as I pass beneath, a twist that allow my wider pelvis to clear the rope without catching.

<<speech "aldric">>Lower.<</speech>>

Hip height. This is where it get interesting. I go to my knees and bend backward, passing under the rope in a movement that three months ago would have been physically impossible — my spine simply did not flex that far. Now it do. The alchemy has loosened my joints, lengthened my tendons, gifted me a flexibility that feel almost obscene in its depth.

Aldric watch with his arms crossed, his expression unreadable.

<<speech "aldric">>Your flexibility has roughly doubled since the potions began. Spine, hips, knees, ankles — everything is looser, more mobile. This is a significant combat advantage. Women are naturally more flexible than men, and the potions have given you that and then some. You can dodge angles that would break a male fighter's back.<</speech>>

He pause.

<<speech "aldric">>But it come with a cost. Looser joints mean less stability under impact. A heavy blow that a rigid male frame might absorb, your body will now deflect — or buckle. We must teach you to use the flexibility offensively, not depend on rigidity for defense. You are not a wall anymore. You are a river. Walls break. Rivers flow around what would break them.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>A river. You compare me to water now.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>I compare you to what will keep you alive. If poetry bother you, I can compare you to a well-oiled gate hinge instead.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>River is fine.<</speech>>


/* ── SCENE 5: THE GRIP TEST — HANDS ── */

Next, Aldric hand me a thick rope knotted to a wooden beam overhead.

<<speech "aldric">>Hang. As long as you can.<</speech>>

I reach up and grip the rope. My hands — I look at them as they wrap around the hemp. My hands. They are... smaller. The fingers thinner, the palms narrower, the skin softer. I have been trying not to notice. Trying to pretend that the rings that have grown loose and the gloves that have grown baggy are coincidences rather than evidence.

They are not coincidences.

I hang. The rope bite into my palms — harder than it should, because there is less callous there now, the old warrior's roughness fading week by week as new skin grow in smoother, softer. My grip hold for twenty seconds. Thirty. At forty, my fingers begin to slip. The thinner digits cannot maintain the same crushing pressure I once wielded. At fifty seconds, I drop.

Aldric mark the time.

<<speech "aldric">>Down from your old time by nearly half. Grip strength is one of the first casualties of the transformation. Smaller hands, thinner tendons, less forearm mass. This is why the longsword is becoming a problem — it was designed for a grip you no longer have.<</speech>>

He reach into the leather sack beside the table and produce two objects: short, curved daggers with wrapped hilts, the blades no longer than my forearm.

<<speech "aldric">>These are practice queensblades. Lighter. Shorter. Designed for hands exactly like yours are becoming. We will not use them yet — that come later — but I want you to hold them. Feel the weight.<</speech>>

I take one in each hand. The difference is immediate. Where the longsword felt like dragging an anchor, these feel like extensions of my fingers. Light. Quick. Alive. The hilts are slimmer, contoured for a narrower palm, and when I wrap my hand around one, my fingers close completely, firmly, with none of the desperate stretching that the longsword's grip now demand.

<<speech "mc">>They feel... right. Like they were made for—<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>For a woman's hand. Because they were. Queen Elyria Thornrose commissioned the first pair three hundred years ago, after she watched her sister die trying to wield a man's blade with a woman's grip. Every warrior-queen since has carried them. And when the time come, so shall you.<</speech>>

He take them back and set them on the table. I stare at them. Two daggers, gleaming in the morning light, waiting for hands that are still becoming what they need to be.


/* ── SCENE 6: THE WEIGHT AND BALANCE TEST ── */

<<speech "aldric">>On the scale.<</speech>>

There is a balance scale at the far end of the yard — a heavy wooden platform with a counterweight system, the kind used for measuring supply loads. I step onto it.

Aldric adjust the counterweights, sliding iron blocks along the beam until it balance.

<<speech "aldric">>You have lost eleven pounds since we last weighed you three months ago. But your height has not changed. The weight has not vanished — it has moved. From your shoulders, arms, and upper back, down to your hips, thighs, and...<</speech>>

He glance at my chest. Look away.

<<speech "aldric">>...and lower. The distribution is reshaping your silhouette entirely. You are lighter on top and heavier on the bottom. This is why your old fighting stance feel like standing on a ship in storm — the ballast has shifted.<</speech>>

He make me stand on one foot. Left, then right. On my right foot, I wobble badly — the wider hip throw my balance off, and without the counterweight of a broad male shoulder, my upper body sway.

<<speech "aldric">>Your balance is worst on the right. That will be your weak side in combat until we retrain it. Every opponent who fight you will exploit it if they are clever enough to see it. We must make sure they are not.<</speech>>

On my left foot, I am steadier. Aldric note this.

<<speech "aldric">>Better. Your body is already compensating on the left — the muscles in your left hip and thigh are adapting faster. This is common in transformation. The body do not change evenly. One side will always lead, and the other will follow. We train the weak side twice as hard.<</speech>>


/* ── SCENE 7: THALARA'S VISIT ── */

The sound of soft footsteps. The scent of moonflower and cold silver. I turn to find Archmage Thalara Moonveil standing at the garden archway, her lavender-white hair floating gently as though stirred by a breeze that touch no one else. Her robes shimmer in the morning light and her eyes — amber today, which mean she is curious — are fixed upon me with that ancient, maternal warmth that always make me feel simultaneously comforted and examined.

<<speech "thalara">>I hope I am not intruding, Sir Aldric. I heard you were conducting an assessment. I thought my observations might be of use.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>You are always intruding, Archmage. But you are usually useful enough to justify it. Come.<</speech>>

Thalara glide across the yard — she do not walk so much as drift, her bare feet barely touching the earth — and come to stand before me. She reach up and cup my jaw in one cool hand, turning my face left, then right.

<<speech "thalara">>The facial restructuring is progressing well. The mandible is narrowing — see here, Sir Aldric, the angle of the jaw? Less square, more tapered. The brow ridge is softening. And the adipose tissue in the cheeks is redistributing, which will give the face a rounder, more youthful appearance in the coming weeks.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>I do not care about the cheeks. I care about whether the jaw can take a punch.<</speech>>

<<speech "thalara">>A fair concern. The bone density is decreasing somewhat, as is typical. A blow to the jaw will be more dangerous than it was. However, the lighter skull weight and more flexible neck musculature mean Caelan will be better able to roll with impacts rather than absorb them. Different strategy, comparable survival rate.<</speech>>

She release my jaw and move her hands to my shoulders, pressing with her thumbs.

<<speech "thalara">>The deltoid atrophy is within expected parameters. The muscle is not vanishing — it is restructuring. Longer fibers, less bulk. Caelan will not be able to brute-force a strike the way a male warrior can, but the speed of the muscle contraction should increase. Fast-twitch over slow-twitch. Rapier over warhammer.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>What about the core?<</speech>>

<<speech "thalara">>May I?<</speech>>

She look at me. I nod. She place both hands flat against my stomach, over the tunic, pressing gently. Her fingers trace the line of my obliques, the edge of my abdominals — muscles that are still present but reshaping, pulling inward, creating that hourglass compression that is visible even through fabric.

<<speech "thalara">>The core is strong. The waist narrowing is primarily skeletal — the lower ribs are drawing inward — and muscular redistribution. The abdominals are intact but the profile has changed. You'll notice, Caelan, that your power now generate differently. Less from the shoulders and back, more from here—<</speech>>

She press against my lower abdomen, just above the hip bones.

<<speech "thalara">>—and here.<</speech>>

Her hand move to my hip, pressing the muscle at the side of my pelvis.

<<speech "thalara">>The gluteal complex and the hip rotators are growing significantly. This is where your combat power will live now — in the rotation, the twist, the torque generated by a wider pelvis and stronger hip girdle. Sir Aldric, if you train Caelan to fight from the hips rather than the shoulders, you will find the power loss is far less than you fear.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>You're the second woman to tell me about the hips today. Beginning to think I'm the one who's been doing it wrong for thirty years.<</speech>>

<<speech "thalara">>Perhaps you have. The warrior-queens of the Silver Age fought exclusively from the hip rotation. Their technique was considered inferior by male swordmasters until Queen Thessa cut through six of them in a single duel and the debate rather settled itself.<</speech>>

Aldric grunt. It might be amusement. It might be indigestion. With Aldric, the two are often indistinguishable.

Thalara turn to me, her expression shifting from clinical to something softer.

<<speech "thalara">>How are you feeling, Caelan? Truly. Not the brave answer. The real one.<</speech>>

I open my mouth to give the brave answer. Then I close it. Thalara's eyes change color — from amber to a gentle, pale blue — and I know she already see the truth.

<<speech "mc">>Everything is different. I reach for a cup and my hand is smaller than I expect. I walk down a corridor and my hips bump the wall because I have not yet learned how wide I am. I lie in bed at night and I can feel... things growing. Shifting. The ache in my chest, in my hips, in places I don't want to talk about. My skin is sensitive to everything — the wind, the sheets, a glance. I am living in a house that someone is renovating while I'm still inside it, and every morning I wake up and the rooms have moved.<</speech>>

Thalara place her hand on my cheek. Her skin is cool as moonlight.

<<speech "thalara">>That is the most honest thing you have said to anyone in weeks. Hold onto that honesty, Caelan. The body can endure anything if the spirit do not lie to itself about the cost.<</speech>>

She turn to Aldric.

<<speech "thalara">>The next potion — the chest and torso continuation — is scheduled for three days hence. The changes will accelerate. The breast tissue will enter its primary growth phase. I tell you this so you can plan accordingly.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Define accordingly.<</speech>>

<<speech "thalara">>Soreness. Swelling. Significantly increased sensitivity. Impact to the chest will be painful in a way it has not been before. And the weight... it will become a factor. Not merely cosmetic. Structural. It will change how Caelan carry the upper body, how the spine align, how armor sit. You may wish to consult with Veletia about support garments before—<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>I am not consulting with Veletia about undergarments.<</speech>>

<<speech "thalara">>Then I shall consult with her on your behalf. Caelan's comfort during training is not vanity, Sir Aldric. It is functionality. A warrior distracted by pain in the chest is a warrior with a blade in the belly.<</speech>>

Aldric look as though he would rather fight the entire goblin nation bare-handed than continue this conversation. He wave a hand.

<<speech "aldric">>Fine. Consult. Garments. Whatever is needed. But the training do not stop. Not for tenderness, not for growth, not for any part of this... process.<</speech>>

Thalara smile — that ancient, knowing smile that make me feel like a child and a queen simultaneously.

<<speech "thalara">>I would not dream of stopping it. The body and the blade must learn together. That is the oldest magic there is.<</speech>>

She drift toward the archway, pausing to look back at me once more.

<<speech "thalara">>You are doing beautifully, Caelan. Even when it do not feel beautiful. Especially then.<</speech>>

She vanish through the arch like mist burning off a lake.


/* ── SCENE 8: THE WEIGHTED RUN ── */

The yard is quieter now. Just Aldric and me and the morning sun climbing the walls. He has filled an entire sheet of parchment with measurements, notes, observations. He roll it up and tuck it into his belt.

<<speech "aldric">>One final test. Run the perimeter again. Five laps. But this time—<</speech>>

He remove his leather jerkin and hold it out to me.

<<speech "aldric">>—wear this. Weighted.<</speech>>

The jerkin is lined with thin iron plates sewn into pockets. I pull it on over my tunic. The weight settle onto my shoulders, my chest, my core — perhaps twenty additional pounds. The effect is immediate. The jerkin press down upon my chest and I feel the breast band compress further, the new flesh flattening painfully against my ribs. The weight on my shoulders — shoulders that have been losing mass for months — feel crushing.

<<speech "mc">>Aldric, this is—<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Armor. This is what armor feel like on a body like yours. You need to know. Because the day you ride into battle, you will carry more than this. Run.<</speech>>

I run.

The first lap is agony. The weight drag at every part of me that has grown lighter, softer, less equipped to carry burden. The iron plates press against my chest with each stride, bouncing against the breast band, and I feel each impact as a sharp, jarring ache that lance through the tender tissue. My shoulders burn. My lower back scream.

But my legs — my legs are strong. The new muscle distribution, the wider hips, the powerful glutes that Thalara spoke of — they drive me forward with a force that surprise me. I am slower, yes, but I am not weak. The power is simply... elsewhere now. Lower. Deeper.

By the third lap I have found a rhythm. The iron still hurt but I have learned, in three laps, to adjust my posture — leaning slightly forward, letting the weight settle through my core rather than hanging from my shoulders. A woman's carry. A woman's balance.

By the fifth lap, I am not fast but I am steady. And steady, as Aldric once told me years ago, is what win wars.

I stop before him, bent double, sweat dripping from my chin, my chest heaving against the iron plates.

<<speech "aldric">>How do the legs feel?<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Strong. Stronger than I expected.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>And the chest?<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Like someone is pressing hot irons against me.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Then we know what to fix. The lower body is ahead of the upper. The legs have adapted to the changes faster. We build from the strength upward. Your legs will be your foundation. Your hips will be your engine. Your arms and chest... we will make accommodations.<</speech>>

He take the weighted jerkin back and I nearly stagger with relief as the pressure lift from my chest. I pull the tunic away from my body and look down — the breast band has shifted during the run, the fabric bunching and riding up, and through the tunic I can see the outline of flesh that has been compressed and jostled and punished for an hour. It ache. A deep, swollen, pulsing ache that throb with my heartbeat.

Aldric see me wince and, for the first time, something that might be regret cross his face.

<<speech "aldric">>I will speak to... to Veletia. Or the Archmage. About proper support for training. What you're wearing now is insufficient. You need something that protect as well as bind. I'll not have you injured by your own... by...<</speech>>

He trail off, clearly wrestling with vocabulary he has never needed and never wanted.

<<speech "mc">>By my own breasts, Aldric. The word is breasts. I have them now. Small, still, but apparently large enough to be a tactical concern. You may as well say it.<</speech>>

He look at me for a long moment. His jaw work.

<<speech "aldric">>...By your own breasts, then. Proper support will be arranged. Dismissed for today.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Aldric.<</speech>>

He stop.

<<speech "mc">>Thank you. For... the numbers. The measurements. All of it. It is easier to face something when it has been named and counted. Even if the count is... unflattering.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>There is nothing unflattering about it, Your Grace. There is nothing flattering either. There is only what is. And what is... is a body in transition, with strengths it do not know and weaknesses it has not yet accepted. My job is to find both before your enemies do.<</speech>>

He turn toward the armoury.

<<speech "aldric">>Tomorrow. First light. We begin the new stance work. Eat well tonight — you'll need the energy.<</speech>>

He pause at the gate.

<<speech "aldric">>And tell Maera her breast band work is passable, but she tie the knot too high. A hand's width lower and it will not ride up during exertion. I noticed it shifting on your fourth lap.<</speech>>

My mouth fall open. He had *seen* it. Through the tunic, through the weighted jerkin, through all of it — he had seen the band, noted its construction, identified its maker, and diagnosed its flaw. Nothing escape the man. Nothing.

<<speech "mc">>I... I will tell her.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Do that. First light, Your Grace. Do not be late.<</speech>>

He disappear through the gate. The clang of the armoury door echo across the empty yard.


/* ── SCENE 9: THE BATH — MAERA'S RETURN ── */

I return to my chamber and strip. Everything come off — tunic, breeches, boots, breast band, smallclothes. I stand naked in my chamber and I am shaking, though whether from exertion or cold or something deeper I cannot say.

I run a bath. The water is hot and I sink into it with a groan that start in my bones and end somewhere near my soul. The heat envelop me and I feel every bruise, every chafe, every ache that the morning has left behind.

The breast band has left red marks across my chest — angry lines where the fabric pressed too tight, the skin beneath them flushed and irritated. I touch the marks gently and hiss at the tenderness. The flesh itself — the new breast tissue, such as it is — is swollen from the compression and the impacts. I cup my hands over them in the water and feel them float slightly, buoyed by the warmth, and the sensation is so bizarre and so intimate that a sound escape me — not pain, not pleasure, something in between, the sound of a body discovering what it is becoming.

My thighs are rubbed raw at the inner seam where they pressed together during the running. The skin there is pink and hot. I press my knees apart in the water and let the heat soothe the friction burns, and I notice — as I have been noticing, as I cannot stop noticing — that even with my knees apart, the thighs still touch. There is no longer a gap. The flesh has filled in, soft and warm, and when I bring my legs together, the contact is... complete. Full. A woman's thighs, pressed together from hip to knee.

I slide deeper into the bath until the water reach my chin. My hair float around my shoulders — dark tendrils on the surface, longer than they have ever been. My body is submerged, visible only as shapes beneath the water — the curve of breast, the line of waist, the flare of hip.

I close my eyes.

Aldric's numbers echo in my mind. Three inches on the hips. Four off the waist. Eleven pounds redistributed. Grip strength halved. Flexibility doubled.

I am being taken apart and rebuilt. Not by an enemy. Not by force. By my own choice, my own alchemy, my own relentless, terrifying becoming.

A knock at the chamber door.

<<speech "maera">>Caelan? Aldric said to tell you the knot was too high. What does that mean? What knot? Why is a fifty-year-old knight critiquing my needlework?<</speech>>

I laugh. It come out of me before I can stop it — a real laugh, warm and sudden, bubbling up from somewhere deep.

<<speech "mc">>Come in. I'll explain. But I'm in the bath, so don't—<</speech>>

The door open.

<<speech "maera">>Nothing I haven't seen before. Or... well. Some things I haven't seen before, apparently. But that's rather the whole point, isn't it?<</speech>>

She sit on the stool beside the tub and her eyes flicker to the water — to the shapes visible just beneath the surface. She look away quickly, but not before I catch the expression that cross her face. Not shock. Not disgust. Something closer to wonder, carefully hidden behind a mask of practicality.

I tell her about the morning. The measurements. The running. The hip rotation. Veletia and her tea. Thalara and her moonlight hands. Aldric and his brutal, precise mercy.

Maera listen. She do not interrupt. When I finish, she reach into the water and fish out my hand — my smaller, thinner, softer hand — and hold it.

<<speech "maera">>Eleven pounds, huh?<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Eleven pounds.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>Redistributed to where, exactly?<</speech>>

She glance pointedly at my chest, which is visible just above the waterline. The small mounds break the surface like two islands in a warm sea, pink and swollen from the morning's abuse.

<<speech "mc">>Everywhere, Maera. It has gone everywhere.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>Well. At least it's going to good places.<</speech>>

I splash her. She shriek and recoil.

<<speech "maera">>I'll fix the knot, you ungrateful wretch! A hand's width lower, you said?<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>That is what the old man said.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>Then the old man know his way around a breast band, and I am not going to think about what that imply. I'll have a better one for you by tomorrow morning. Thicker fabric. Lower tie. And I'll add a second layer at the front — you're going to need it, by the sound of things.<</speech>>

She stand, shaking water droplets from her sleeves, and move toward the door. She stop with her hand on the latch.

<<speech "maera">>Caelan?<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Yes?<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>The way you described the running. The last two laps. When you stopped fighting and let your body move the way it want. That sound... I don't know. That sound like something good. Like the beginning of something, instead of the end of something.<</speech>>

I look at her. Maera, with her sharp eyes and her crooked grin and her hands that made me a breast band without being asked because she saw me suffering and could not bear it.

<<speech "mc">>I think it might be both. The end of something and the beginning of something, at the same time. I just don't know what to call either of them yet.<</speech>>

<<speech "maera">>You'll figure it out. You always do. Usually the hard way, but you get there.<</speech>>

She smile. She close the door. And I sink back into the warm water and close my eyes and let the ache settle into every new curve and hollow of this body that is mine, that is changing, that is — despite everything, despite the fear and the numbers and the iron plates against my chest — learning to run.

Tomorrow. First light. The new stance.

I will be there.


/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
/*  END OF DAY 2                                               */
